SLAM Quick Reference
====================
While SLAM can generate documentation using nothing more than plain text comments, it also offers a simple markup language that developers can use to define document structure, formatting, and cross-references. The following sections explain how to use this language:

Basic Principles
Paragraph Formatting
Character Formatting
Links
Preview
In addition, two scripts serve as exemplars of the various markup directives: _LIB SLAM Markup Example and _TYPE SLAM Markup Example.

Note: In this document, unless identified otherwise, the term "script" refers to both Slang scripts and C++ source files.

CRITICAL: Spacing Rule
----------------------
All SLAM markup MUST be exactly 2 spaces (or 1 tab) after ** or //.

  CORRECT:
    **  === Heading ===
    **  *  Bullet point
    **  > Code snippet

  WRONG:
    ** === Heading ===
    ** *  Bullet point

This applies to ALL markup and text: headings, lists, code blocks, tables, links, etc.

CRITICAL: Heading Hierarchy
---------------------------
Global headings (== and ===) must be strictly nested. You cannot have a level-2
heading (===) without a preceding level-1 heading (==).

  Module description block:
    **  == Main Title ==           (level 1 - use once at top)
    **  === Sub-Section ===        (level 2 - must follow level 1)

  Routine/code block comments:
    **  ~~Local Heading~~          (first-level, scoped to this block)
    **  ~~~Sub-Heading~~~          (second-level within the block)

Use ~~ headings for routine comments - they don't participate in global hierarchy
and won't cause nesting errors.

Basic Principles
SLAM pages are generated from Add-In/function/Typed Structure signatures, as well as comments of either the in-line ( //) or block ( /*) style. Each page is divided into three parts: Contents, Description, and Interface. The text within these parts is determined as follows:

"Contents" is based on the section headings within your script. These headings are generally used to break Add-In, function, and Typed Structure definitions into logical groupings. They are defined as follows:
// == Level 1 Heading ==
// === Level 2 Heading ===
// ==== Level 3 Heading ====
// ===== Level 4 Heading =====
//        ... Level N Heading ...

You can also add headings to introduce topics inside a routine. Example:
~~ Heading 1 ~~
~~~ Heading 2 ~~~
"Description" is taken from the "Description" field in the script's header.
"Interface" is compiled from Add-In/function/Typed Structure comments and signatures.
"Attributes" in typed structures are not SLAMmed at the moment
Old Style vs. New Style Function Comment Blocks
In Slang, the contents of a function comment block are SLAMmed only if there is a blank line following the routine name. For example:

 /****************************************************************
 **  Routine: Very::Useful
 **
 **  This line will be SLAMmed. Simple as that.
 ****************************************************************/
Anything that does not include a blank line will be treated as an old-style comment block. The contents of these blocks will not be examined for mark-ups and will appear in the SLAM documentation as "<pre>...</pre>" (monospaced) blocks. For example:

 /****************************************************************
 **  Routine: Almost::Completely Useless
 **  Returns:
 **  Summary:
 **  Action :
 ****************************************************************/
In C++ Add-In code, only comments in the newly created Doc: field will be processed for inclusion in SLAM documentation. For example:

/****************************************************************
**  Adlib Name:     Something::Really Fast
**  Doc:    This line will be SLAMmed. Simple as that.
****************************************************************/
Paragraph Formatting
There are several ways to format paragraphs in SLAM (Notice that these examples are from Slang code; the same mark-ups would work within a C++ Add-In's Doc: field):

Line breaks - <br> inserts a new blank line
Blank Lines - Any text preceded by a blank line will be rendered as a new paragraph. Example:
 /****************************************************************
 **  Routine: Very::Useful
 **
 **  This is the first paragraph. It contains two sentences.
 **
 **  This is the second paragraph. It contains three sentences.
 **  See?
 ****************************************************************/
Code Snippets - Any text preceded by a greater-than sign will be rendered as a code snippet (to use the greater-than sign in text, escape it with a backslash: \>). Example:
 /****************************************************************
 **  Routine: Very::Useful
 **
 **  The following code explains how to do something:
 **  > // how to solve a*x*x + b*x + c = 0
 **  > t = b * b - 4 * a * c;
 **  > If( t \> 0 )
 **  > {
 **  >     s = Sqrt( t );
 **  >     root1 = ( -b + s ) / 2 * a;
 **  >     root2 = ( -b - s ) / 2 * a;
 **  > }
 **  > :
 **  >     Throw( "roots are complex!" );
 **
 **  Now, let's talk about something else in a new paragraph...
 ****************************************************************/
Lists - Any line starting with an asterisk will be rendered as a bulleted list (to use the asterisk in text, escape it with a backslash: \*). And a line starting with a pound/hash will be rendered as a numbered list (to use the pound/hash in text, use: \#). The number of symbols determine the level. Finally we have a definition list, which starts a line with a semi-colon (;) and deliminates the term and description with a colon (:). Examples of each:
 /****************************************************************
 **  Routine: Very::Useful
 **
 **  Bulleted list:
 **  * Apples
 **  ** Bananas
 **  * Cherries
 **  This is new paragraph following a bulleted list. Now a numbered
 **  list:
 **  # Apricots
 **  ## Peaches
 **  # Nectarines
 **  This is new paragraph following a numbered list. Now a nested numbered/bullet list:
 **  # Grapes
 **  #* Red
 **  #* Green
 **  # Kiwis
 **  This is new paragraph following a numbered list. Finally, a
 **  definition list:
 **  ; Pluot : a hybrid of a plum and apricot, being 2/3 plum and 1/3 apricot
 **  ; Aprium : a hybrid of a plum and apricot, being 2/3 apricot and 1/3 plum
 **  ; Peacotum : a hybrid of peach, apricot and plum
 ****************************************************************/
The definition list format is a nice way to format comments in code reviews. In particular, you can type:


; FileOne.cpp :
* Do not use c-style casts.
* Check return value of DtUnwrap.

; FileTwo.cpp :
* Please add a comment explaining the return value of my_cool_function.

CAUTION: Mixing List Types
--------------------------
Definition lists (; term : desc) cannot have nested bullet lists (**) as
sub-items. The list types do not mix well.

  WRONG (bullets won't render as sub-items of definition):
    **  ; Deep Coerce Types : Controls date/time coercion
    **  ** Null::Structure - Disabled
    **  ** Structure() - Auto-coerce date/time strings
    **  ; Other Field : Description

  The ** bullets will not appear as children of the definition item.
  They may render incorrectly or break the list structure.

  WORKAROUNDS:
  1. Put all info in the definition description (inline):
      **  ; Deep Coerce Types : Controls date/time coercion. Values: Null::Structure (disabled), Structure() (auto-coerce)

  2. Use bullet lists consistently instead of definition lists:
      **  * Deep Coerce Types - Controls date/time coercion
     **  ** Null::Structure - Disabled
      **  ** Structure() - Auto-coerce date/time strings
     **  * Other Field - Description

  3. Use separate definition entries (no nesting):
      **  ; Deep Coerce Types : Controls date/time coercion
     **  ; Deep Coerce Types (disabled) : Use Null::Structure
     **  ; Deep Coerce Types (auto) : Use Structure()

  RECOMMENDATION: For API documentation with sub-items, prefer bullet lists (*)
  for consistency and predictable rendering. Definition lists work best for
  simple term/description pairs without nested content.

Multi-line monospace/pre-formatted - Use the <pre> tag to render a multi-line block of text in a monospace font. Example:
<pre>
This is a block
of text.
		 It will
		 be printed
		 as it appears
onscreen.
</pre>
Result:
This is a block
of text.
		 It will
		 be printed
		 as it appears
onscreen.
Character Formatting
The text within a comment can include any of the following formatting directives.

Emphasis - Use two single quotes to italicise text, three quotes to bold text, and five to bold-italic text. (To display quotes in text, escape them with a backslash: \'\'). Example:
 // This is ''italics'' text, '''bold''' text, '''''bolditalic''''' text
Monospace - Use the <tt> tag to render text in a monospace font. Example:
 // This is <tt>monospace</tt> text.
Strikethrough - Use the <strike> tag to show text with a strikethrough. Example:
 // This is <strike>strikethrough</strike> text.

CRITICAL: Escaping Angle Brackets
---------------------------------
Angle brackets that are NOT valid SLAM tags will cause "LEX-ING FAILED" errors.
You MUST escape literal angle brackets with backslashes.

  Valid SLAM tags (do NOT escape):
    <tt>, </tt>, <pre>, </pre>, <br>, <math>, </math>,
    <strike>, </strike>, <table>, <tr>, <td>, <th>,
    <quote>, </quote>, <out>, </out>, <graph>, </graph>

  WRONG (causes lexing error):
    **  "Missing parameter: '<name>'"
    **  > {| "key" := <default value> |}
    **  The function returns <something useful>

  CORRECT (escaped):
    **  "Missing parameter: '\<name\>'"
    **  > {| "key" := \<default value\> |}
    **  The function returns \<something useful\>

This is a common source of SLAM errors - any text that looks like an HTML/XML
tag but isn't a recognized SLAM tag will break the lexer.


CRITICAL: <tt> Tags Do NOT Fully Protect Content
------------------------------------------------
A common misconception is that <tt>...</tt> will prevent SLAM from parsing
the content inside. THIS IS FALSE. The <tt> tag only changes the font to
monospace - SLAM still parses the content for markup.

  SAFE: Use > code snippets for Slang syntax examples:
    **  >    MyVar = {| "key" := "value" |};

  Code snippets (>) provide true protection from SLAM parsing and also
  give you syntax highlighting and hyperlinks to functions. 
  Intenting the whole code block by a 3 extra spaces makes the slang file doc more readable outside of slam

Math - Use the <math> tag to specify that a string is a formula. Documentation explaining how to write formulae is available from Wikipedia. Example:
 /**********************************************************
 **  Routine: Very::Useful
 **
 **  If I said our ansatz for the spacetime metric is
 **  <math>ds^2 = - dt^2 + dr^2 + \left[ r - \lambda f(r) \right]^2d\Omega^2,
 **  \quad 0 \leq \lambda f </math>
 **  then you might want to talk about a Gaussian bowl:
 **
 **  <math>f(r) = f_{\rm G} = e^{-(r-r_0)^2/\sigma^2}</math>
 **********************************************************/
Links
To create links (either between SLAM documents or to outside pages), use the following directives:

Script/FAQ - Use double square-brackets to link to a script or FAQ. If you'd like to specify link text, use a pipe. Otherwise, the link will contain the name of the script or the FAQ Question. Example:
 /**********************************************************
 **  Routine: Very::Useful
 **
 **  See [[_LIB Array Functions]] or
 **  [[_LIB Date Functions|Slang's library of date math routines]].
 **  Alternatively, take a look at [[FAQE Doctor, it hurts I dS7N  0]]
 **  or [[FAQE store LaTeX documentELY  0|the LaTeX FAQ]].
 **********************************************************/
Add-In/Function/Typed Structure - Use double square-brackets and the @ sign to link to Add-Ins/functions/Typed Structures. If you'd like to specify link text, use a pipe. Example:
 /**********************************************************
 **  Routine: Very::Useful
 **
 **  If you want to build a foobar, call [[@Foobar::Build]].
 **  If you want to build a barfoo, call the
 **  [[@Barfoo::Build|barfoo builder function]].
 **********************************************************/
Notice that you can link to Add-Ins, functions, and Typed Structures in the current script or in any other script. If your link cannot be resolved to an actual Add-In, function, or Typed Structure, it will be rendered in monospace.
URL - Use single square-brackets to link to a URL. If you'd like to specify link text, place a space after the URL, and then enter the text. Prefix with "URL:" to use a relative URL (not so useful in scripts, more so in FAQs). You can also use this to have a mailto: or other types of URL (e.g. FTP). Example:
 /**********************************************************
 **  Routine: Very::Useful
 **
 **  To search the Web, go to [http://www.google.com].
 **  To search Goldman Sachs Web pages, go to
 **  [http://home.gs.com/gsweb/gsc GSWeb Home Page].
 **  Here's a relative URL: [URL:foo/bar.html a relative link]
 **  If you want to know more, mail [URL:mailto:gs-secdb-help@ny.email.gs.com GS SecDB Help]
 **********************************************************/
Issue tracker item - Use double square-brackets and "IS:" prefix to link to an issue item by number. Mostly useful in SLAM-d review requests. Example:
 Please review my fix for [[IS:1234-5678]], which I believe solves the problem nicely.
MetaDir(ectory) - Use double square-brackets and "metadir:" prefix to link to kerberos ids, dept codes or mailing lists. If you'd like to specify link text, place a space after the name, and then enter the text. Example:
 /**********************************************************
 **  Routine: Very::Useful
 **
 **  You can refer to [[metadir:walkerpa Paul]],
 **  [[metadir:ficcs-st-core the core team]], or
 **  [[metadir:F581 core users]].
 **********************************************************/
Yams Initials - Use double square-brackets and "yams:" or "y:" prefix to show the Yams initials of the given kerberos id. Example:
 /**********************************************************
 **  Routine: Very::Useful
 **
 **  You can refer to [[yams:walkerpa]] or [[y:goodfj]].
 **********************************************************/
Images - Use double square-brackets and "Image:" prefix to link to images. First you need to upload the image so SLAM can access it. Use _UT SLAM Upload Image to upload the image to the database.The file must obviously be in graphic format (i.e. GIF, JPEG, PNG) and less than 64Kb so that we don't jam up the database with pictures. Example:
 /**********************************************************
 **  Routine: Very::Useful
 **
 **  You can refer to [[Image:mygraphic.gif]],
 **  [[Image:mygraphic.gif|My Graphic]] with alternate text, or
 **  [[Image:mygraphic.gif|center|My Centred Graphic]] center aligned
 **  with alternate text.
 **********************************************************/
Procmon - You can link to a procnosticator job easily. Just specify [[proc:master:jobname]] Example:
 [[proc:cred:cd/tko/srv/prod/sds_addressbook_publisher_prod]]
You can also add other URL attributes after the job name:
 [[proc:cred:cd/tko/srv/prod/sds_addressbook_publisher_prod&Location=TKO]]
Script Review - You can link to a specific code review without specifying the full url . Just specify [[Review Date ID]] Example:
 [[Review 20121127 6010-392072]]
JSI File - Use the following syntax: [[jsi:module-name:relative path to the module]]. Example:
 [[jsi:jsi-version-control:src/com/gs/jsi/core/versioncontrol/MergeProvider.scala]]
CVS file - use the following syntax: [[cvs:procmon/procs/a/b/c]]. You can optionally add a revision in particular by adding it after a colon. Example:
 [[cvs:ficc/liberty/src/secdat/ficclibertysettings.dat:1.1]]
